{%- comment -%}
  Analytics. Every provider registers itself as a *sink* behind its own theme
  setting, and window.bfTrack(eventName, params) fans each event out to all of
  them — sections never call gtag()/posthog directly. Shopify's Customer Privacy
  API is the consent source of record and is synced to every provider once,
  here, rather than once per vendor.

  Three parts, in order:
    1. Liquid-rendered config — the only Liquid that reaches JavaScript.
    2. Vendor loaders, each emitted only when configured and never in the
       theme editor (request.design_mode).
    3. The logic script, which carries no Liquid at all so that
       test/analytics.test.js can extract and execute it verbatim.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script>
  window.bfAnalyticsConfig = {
    ga4: {{ settings.ga4_measurement_id | json }},
    posthogKey: {{ settings.posthog_project_key | json }},
    posthogHost: {{ settings.posthog_api_host | default: 'https://us.i.posthog.com' | json }},
    designMode: {{ request.design_mode | json }}
  };
</script>
{%- if settings.ga4_measurement_id != blank and request.design_mode == false -%}
  <script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id={{ settings.ga4_measurement_id }}"></script>
{%- endif -%}
{%- if settings.posthog_project_key != blank and request.design_mode == false -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    PostHog's official loader stub (posthog.com/docs/libraries/js). It queues
    every listed method — init, capture, opt_in_capturing, opt_out_capturing —
    until array.js arrives, so the logic script below can call them at once.
    The asset host is derived from api_host (us.i → us-assets.i).
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script>
